{{/*
family: line-cli
what: trap
transports: v2c, v3
reference: common line-oriented SNMP commands (snmp-server host, snmp-server enable traps, snmp-server group, snmp-server user); RFC 3414 for the v3 user model
verification: reference
*/}}
{{define "does"}}Sends the device's SNMP traps to Woodshed (SNMP{{.Transport}}).{{end}}

{{define "parts"}}
@@part cli - Enter in global configuration mode, then save the configuration
{{- if eq .Transport "v3"}}
snmp-server group woodshed v3 priv
snmp-server user {{.V3User}} woodshed v3 auth {{.Auth.Line}} <AUTH-PASSPHRASE> priv {{.Priv.Line}} <PRIV-PASSPHRASE>
snmp-server host {{.Address}} version 3 priv {{.V3User}}{{if ne .Port 162}} udp-port {{.Port}}{{end}}
{{- else}}
snmp-server host {{.Address}} version 2c <COMMUNITY>{{if ne .Port 162}} udp-port {{.Port}}{{end}}
{{- end}}
snmp-server enable traps
{{- if .SourceInterface}}
snmp-server trap-source {{.SourceInterface}}{{end}}
{{end}}

{{define "notes"}}
{{if eq .Transport "v2c"}}<COMMUNITY> must be one Woodshed accepts: Settings → Listeners → SNMP traps lists the communities (an empty list accepts any).
{{else}}Add the same user in Woodshed: Settings → SNMP → v3 users, name {{.V3User}}, {{.Auth.Label}} and {{.Priv.Label}}, with the same passphrases.
The protocol keywords differ between devices (`sha-256`, `sha256` or `sha2-256`; `aes 128` or `aes128`), and some only offer SHA-1 or put the user's keys under the engine ID: pick the protocols both sides have.
{{end}}
{{- if ne .Port 162}}Woodshed's trap port is {{.Port}}, not the usual 162; some devices name the port with another keyword, or can't send to another port at all.
{{end}}
`snmp-server enable traps` turns on every notification the device has on some devices, and only a default set on others; narrow it to what you need.
{{end}}

{{define "check"}}Search Events for type Traps after a change on the device (an interface going down and up, a configuration save); `woodshed snmp status` counts traps per device.{{end}}
